<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">English Channel</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The English Channel is the arm of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> that separates southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> at the Strait of Dover, about 33 km wide. It has long been a route for trade and migration and a barrier against invasion, crossed by Caesar, by the Normans in 1066 and by the Allied armies in 1944. The Channel Tunnel has linked the two shores since 1994.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/english-channel.jpg" alt="English Channel" width="320" height="168" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joowwww · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:English_Channel_Satellite.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>British and French workers broke through the last wall of rock and shook hands beneath the <b>English Channel</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-1">1 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The White Ship sank in the <b>English Channel</b> off Barfleur, drowning William Adelin, heir to Henry I of England; of some 300 aboard, only a butcher from Rouen survived.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-25">25 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Their coins and their links across the <b>Channel</b> show close ties with Britain, where a people of the same name lived.</blockquote><a href="/state/atrebates">Atrebates</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 50 BCE he fled across the <b>Channel</b>, and coins bearing his name soon appear in central southern Britain, where he appears to have founded a dynasty ruling Hampshire, Berkshire and Sussex.</blockquote><a href="/state/atrebates-britain">Atrebates (Britain)</a></li><li><blockquote>They struck gold coins and controlled the route between the Loire and the <b>Channel</b> coast.</blockquote><a href="/state/aulerci-cenomani">Aulerci Cenomani</a></li><li><blockquote>The Caleti lived on the plateau between the lower Seine and the <b>Channel</b> cliffs.</blockquote><a href="/state/caleti">Caleti</a></li></ul></section>
